      "One of the most common questions fabric buyers ask Indian manufacturers is about minimum order quantity. The honest answer is: it depends on the construction, not on a single fixed number every mill applies uniformly — and understanding why helps you ask the right question when you first inquire.",
      "## Why MOQ Depends on Construction, Not a Fixed Rule",
      "For fabrics already in a manufacturer's running range — constructions they weave regularly as part of their standard offering — MOQ is typically lower, because no new loom setup, yarn procurement, or dye-lot minimum is required beyond what the mill already runs. For custom development — a new construction, a specific blend not currently produced, or a particular finish outside the existing range — MOQ applies from the development stage itself, not just the eventual bulk stage, because the mill is committing setup and yarn-procurement resources specifically for your request.",
      "## What Actually Drives MOQ at an Indian Mill",
      "Several factors compound to set the real minimum: whether the construction is already in the mill's running range (lower MOQ) or requires new development (higher MOQ from the start). Yarn count and composition — specialty yarns often carry their own minimum purchase quantities from the spinning mill supplying the yarn, which becomes a floor under the fabric MOQ regardless of what the weaving mill itself would otherwise accept. The width required — standard widths (44/45 inch and 58/60 inch) generally carry lower minimums than a custom width, since standard widths are already set up on existing looms. And the finish required — dyed and printed fabrics carry minimum batch sizes at the dyeing/printing stage that can end up higher than the weaving MOQ alone.",
